In article <1662@ucqais.uc.edu>, rainwatr@ucqais.uc.edu (Donald J. Rainwater) writes:
> 	Active levels would remain in memory under the dungeon process, and 
> would go away when there were no longer any players on that level.  A level
> that was active for a player that has saved his game will be kept until that
> player resumes and leaves the level.  Of course, a player who leaves the game
> saved runs the risk of the level being cleared out by the time he resumes.

Having a process per player is a nice idea, but it won't work even
single-user under MS-DOS.  Of course, with OS/2 and Lan Manager this
would work just fine, and would do especially well on a network.

Doubtless a multi-player version of Nethack would include lots of
new features (like real-time activity, etc.) that MS-DOS users would
want, too.  Let's not shut them out by using an implementation which
requires the capabilities of an operating system like UN*X or OS/2.

So who's writing the OS/2 PM version of Nethack?

